WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.681
An API is a way for programs to ask each other for information using agreed names and formats.

00:00:07.681 --> 00:00:10.396
Think of a parcel enquiry form.

00:00:10.396 --> 00:00:16.691
The carrier tells you which number goes in the box and what information you can expect back.

00:00:16.691 --> 00:00:22.847
Your application can offer a simple tool that fills out that form behind the scenes.

00:00:22.847 --> 00:00:29.869
This small piece of code translates the model’s request into the carrier’s format and reads the answer.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:32.437
The carrier may send a long record.

00:00:32.437 --> 00:00:39.063
Your tool can return just the useful details, such as status and estimated delivery day.

00:00:39.063 --> 00:00:44.707
A service can also decline a request or ask the application to wait.

00:00:44.707 --> 00:00:47.804
Ana wants to know when her tent will arrive.

00:00:47.804 --> 00:00:54.089
Orbit’s shop uses an order number, but ParcelPath tracks parcels using a different number.

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:56.069
Start with Ana’s order.

00:00:56.069 --> 00:00:59.679
Its record says shipped and gives a tracking number.

00:00:59.679 --> 00:01:06.667
Sending the shop order number directly to the carrier would ask it to find the wrong kind of record.

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:09.978
Here is why Scout needs the carrier's API.

00:01:09.978 --> 00:01:12.596
Ana asks when her tent will arrive.

00:01:12.596 --> 00:01:19.063
Scout calls get order, which returns her tracking number, but not where the parcel is right now.

00:01:19.063 --> 00:01:26.404
So Scout calls a second tool, track parcel, with that tracking number, and that tool calls ParcelPath.

00:01:26.404 --> 00:01:31.621
Now Scout can tell Ana her tent is in transit and due on Thursday.

00:01:31.621 --> 00:01:39.374
Notice that the output of one tool became the input of the next, and the model made that connection itself.

00:01:39.374 --> 00:01:42.293
Pass the tracking number to the carrier tool.

00:01:42.293 --> 00:01:49.380
It goes into the carrier’s enquiry, which returns in transit and an estimated Thursday delivery.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.561
Compare those two records.

00:01:51.561 --> 00:01:56.440
The shop establishes that this tracking number belongs to Ana’s tent.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.909
The carrier supplies the estimate.

00:01:58.909 --> 00:02:03.272
Neither record alone answers the entire question.

00:02:03.272 --> 00:02:06.156
Picture two labels on a parcel.

00:02:06.156 --> 00:02:10.182
One number belongs to the shop and the other to the carrier.

00:02:10.182 --> 00:02:16.114
Starting with the shop's order number is sensible because that is the number Ana knows.

00:02:16.114 --> 00:02:18.660
The carrier expects the other label.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.080
Put the shop result beside the carrier request.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.882
The tracking number on the left must be the one on the right.

00:02:25.882 --> 00:02:32.926
A real delivery estimate for somebody else's parcel would still produce a wrong answer for Ana.

00:02:32.926 --> 00:02:36.727
Read the carrier's wording before drafting the reply.

00:02:36.727 --> 00:02:41.392
An estimate can change, and in transit does not mean delivered.

00:02:41.392 --> 00:02:49.958
Keeping those two distinctions lets Scout give Ana a useful answer without turning a forecast into a guarantee.

00:02:49.958 --> 00:02:52.767
The last habit is trimming the result.

00:02:52.767 --> 00:03:01.174
ParcelPath's full answer is huge, but Scout mostly needs two things: the status and the expected delivery day.

00:03:01.174 --> 00:03:06.712
The last scan helps too, for customers who ask where exactly the parcel is.

00:03:06.712 --> 00:03:11.802
Scout does not need dozens of scan events, depot codes and barcodes.

00:03:11.802 --> 00:03:17.075
And some fields, like a driver's notes, should never reach a customer at all.

00:03:17.075 --> 00:03:26.342
Trimming takes the result from thousands of lines to three fields, which means fewer tokens, lower cost and fewer mistakes.

00:03:26.342 --> 00:03:32.210
Scout can now explain that the parcel is in transit and expected on Thursday.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:35.244
It should describe that day as an estimate.

00:03:35.244 --> 00:03:41.531
If the carrier replies that too many requests arrived, the application waits as directed.

00:03:41.531 --> 00:03:47.815
A delay getting fresh information gives Scout no reason to invent a delivery day.
